        H("1552", "The siege of Eger and Bull's Blood", "István Dobó's small garrison held Eger castle against a vast Ottoman army. A later legend says the defenders drank red wine before battle and the Turks, seeing their stained beards, believed the Hungarians drank bull's blood. The name Bikavér appears only centuries later, but the story sells wine to this day.", 47.9041, 20.3795),
        H("1571", "First written mention of aszú", "In 2002 the historian István Zelenák found aszú grape wine in the 1571 inheritance letter of Máté Garai. Balázs Szikszai Fabricius's dictionary of 1576 also records it. Aszú was therefore a known product two generations before the famous legend.", 48.1235, 21.4114),
        H("1631", "The Oremus legend", "Tradition credits the Reformed pastor Máté Szepsi Laczkó with making an aszú from grapes of the Oremus vineyard at Sátoraljaújhely for Princess Zsuzsanna Lorántffy at Easter. Historians now treat it as legend: Szepsi's own chronicle never mentions aszú. The story still names Tokaj's most mythic vineyard.", 48.3799, 21.6357),
        H("c. 1700 to 1772", "Vineyards ranked by class", "Surveys of the Rákóczi era were followed by János Matolai and Mátyás Bél's three-class ranking in the 1720s and 1730s, completed by the censuses of 1765 and 1772. Sites such as Oremus were rated primae classis. Antal Szirmay refined the ranking again in 1798.", 48.1918, 21.2742),
        H("1703", "Wine of kings, king of wines", "Prince Ferenc II Rákóczi, leading Hungary's war of independence against the Habsburgs, used Tokaji as diplomatic currency and sent it to Louis XIV. The phrase 'Vinum Regum, Rex Vinorum' is traditionally attributed to the French king.", 48.3156, 21.5689),
        H("1737", "A closed wine region", "On 1 October 1737 King Charles VI (Károly III of Hungary) defined which towns could call their wine Tokaji and required permission to plant. The Tokaj product specification calls it the first definition of a protected origin in the world.", 48.1235, 21.4114),
